scope: "Routing the Intelligence Console's 20 in-process data sources off raw `paho` and through `ComposeIOBus`, so WHICH SUBSTRATE carries a message becomes the bus's business and not the app's. Covers the bus-handle seam, EXPLICIT scope declaration (never auto-resolve), the externality classification per namespace, the `run()`-must-block invariant that keeps the supervisor's reclaim loop from mis-firing, the slice order, and the on-the-wire acceptance bar."
audience: "The builder converting the console data sources; the composeux-architect ratifying the open calls; anyone who later adds a data source and must know how a source reaches the bus."
boundary: "IN: how an in-process source acquires a bus handle (injected in-process, self-created standalone); the explicit-scope declaration rule + its conflict-is-loud discipline; the per-source namespace/scope inventory; the wire deltas the routing change legitimately introduces; the `run()`-blocks invariant + its enforcing test; the two-stage sequencing (route first, flip scope second) and the slice order; the acceptance bar. OUT: the ComposeBus protocol itself ([plt.cio:SPEC:CIO-BUS-PROTOCOL] — consumed, never changed); the widgets' binding layer ([SPEC:CXC-BIND]); `intelligence/launcher/launcher.py` (deliberately exempt, [CXC-SOB-EXEMPT]); the standalone out-of-process peers `app_launcher` / `decisions_curator` / `console_supervisor` (they explain WHY `_app/*` is external, they are not converted here); the eventual fold of `bus_envelope`/`bus_client` into `transport_mqtt` (stated as the end-state in [CXC-SOB-ENDSTATE], executed in a later slice)."
not-this: "Not a protocol change — no namespace is renamed, no payload reshaped, no topic moved ([CXC-SOB-CONTRACT-FROZEN]). Not a new uplink-policy layer — `scope` already IS the uplink control and a parallel mechanism was already built and RETRACTED once ([CXC-SOB-NO-POLICY-LAYER]). Not a performance optimization — the connection collapse is a by-product; the goal is substrate independence."
