# Console Data Sources on the Bus [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

> The console's 20 in-process data sources publish with raw `paho` to hand-built
> `composeio/{scope}/{ns}` topics. A source that writes `client.publish(f"composeio/global/{ns}", ...)`
> has hardcoded MQTT; on a CAN-only device it publishes into the void. This spec routes every source
> through `ComposeIOBus` so the app declares a NAMESPACE and a SCOPE and stops naming a substrate — and
> it implements [DEC-INTERNAL-BUS-SELECTIVE-UPLINK], `decided` since 2026-07-22 and unbuilt because the
> bus API used to reject the sources' payloads. That blocker was fixed on 2026-08-08; the bypass has no
> remaining justification.

---

## 1. The thesis and the frozen contract

### [CXC-SOB-THESIS] The app publishes to ComposeBus; the bus decides the substrate

- premise: David, 2026-08-08 — "ComposeBus might for some devices be instantiated as MQTT, and for some
  it might ride on CANBUS. But in both cases the ComposeIO/UX app shouldn't have to care how ComposeBus
  is instantiated — it should just listen to / publish to ComposeBus. The ComposeBus API should be an
  abstraction layer that makes MQTT calls, pushes onto CANBUS, or ties a message to a carrier pigeon."
- requirement: after this work, NO file under `intelligence/publisher/` or `intelligence/tools/` that is
  registered as a console data source MAY import `paho`, construct an `mqtt.Client()`, or compose a
  `composeio/...` topic string. Every publish is `bus.publish_state(ns, value, source=...)`; every
  subscribe is `bus.subscribe(ns, cb)` / `bus.subscribe_pattern(pattern, cb)`. The substrate is chosen
  once, by `composeio.transport_registry`, from a `type:` declaration.
- verify-machine: a grep guard test (`python/tests/render/test_sources_have_no_paho.py`) over the
  registered source modules FAILS on any of: `import paho`, `mqtt.Client(`, the literal `composeio/`,
  `loop_forever(`, `loop_start(`. Exemptions are an explicit allow-list in the test naming
  [CXC-SOB-EXEMPT] and NOTHING else — an unnamed exemption is a red test. This is a CLASS guard in the
  shape [SBR-PATHS-SINGLE-SOURCE] uses: reorg-proofing is a red test, not a convention.
- verify-human: none required — this node is mechanical.
- rationale: the seam already exists and was already proved reachable (`transport_registry`, 2026-08-08,
  after `runtime._build_bus` was found to silently drop `type: can`). The sources are the last consumers
  below the waist that reach past it.
- relates-to: [CXC-SOB-HANDLE], [CXC-SOB-ENDSTATE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-CONTRACT-FROZEN] The bus contract does not move — this is transport routing, not protocol

- invariant: for every namespace in [CXC-SOB-INVENTORY], the following are UNCHANGED by this work:
  the namespace STRING; the payload VALUE shape; the envelope field set and order
  (`ns,type,src,ts,scope,seq,val` — pinned by `bus_envelope._selftest` and produced by the canonical
  `BusMessage.to_json()`); the `src` string each source stamps; and, in Stage A, the resolved SCOPE and
  therefore the MQTT topic. A consumer outside the process MUST be unable to tell the conversion
  happened, except for the deltas enumerated in [CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS].
- invariant: each source keeps its importable `main(argv)` entry point and stays runnable standalone as
  a script with IDENTICAL argv. [DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES] depends on a source being runnable both as a
  console thread and as a script; a conversion that only works in-process breaks the per-source
  testability that decision bought.
- invariant: the per-`<id>` singleton `flock` behaviour ([CXC-IC-SINGLETON], `singleton.py`) is
  untouched. `acquire_singleton` still runs at the TOP of `run()`, before the bus handle is acquired,
  so a yielding instance never opens a transport it is about to abandon.
- verify-machine: [CXC-SOB-ACCEPT] SOB-C2 (capture-diff) and SOB-C6 (argv parity).
- relates-to: [CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS], [CXC-SOB-TWO-STAGE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-EXEMPT] What is deliberately NOT converted, and why

- definition: four exemption classes. Each is named here so the [CXC-SOB-THESIS] grep guard has an
  explicit, reviewable allow-list rather than a silent skip.
  1. **`intelligence/launcher/launcher.py` — permanently exempt.** It hand-frames to keep a paho-only,
     `composeio`-import-free startup footprint. That is a real constraint, not an oversight; do not
     "fix" it. Its `publish_state` already carries a pointer to the envelope seam.
  2. **Out-of-process peers — out of scope, not exempt in principle.** `intelligence/tools/app_launcher.py`,
     `intelligence/tools/decisions_curator.py`, `intelligence/console/console_supervisor.py`,
     `intelligence/host/poc_driver.py`, `intelligence/console/chat.py`. They are separate OS processes,
     not console data sources. They matter here ONLY as evidence of consumer locality
     ([CXC-SOB-EXTERNALITY]) — several of them are the reason `_app/*` is EXTERNAL.
  3. **`intelligence/channel/channel.mjs` — a different runtime.** The Node channel server is the
     Intelligence's harness-side leg; it speaks raw MQTT by construction and is outside this corpus.
  4. **Spawned viewer windows.** Each is its own `RenderApp` process and already reaches the bus through
     `runtime._build_bus`; they need no conversion.
- constraint: an exemption is a LISTED exemption. Adding a source module to the exemption list requires
  a one-line rationale in the test's allow-list; an unexplained entry is a review reject.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

---

## 2. Why the bypass existed, and why the reason is gone

### [CXC-SOB-BYPASS-EXPIRED] The binary auto-register that forced raw paho is FIXED

- prior-defect: `ComposeIOBus.publish_state` auto-registered an unknown namespace with a **binary**
  state engine, which then rejected the real payload — `publish_state("self/context", 0.203)` raised
  `ValueError: Invalid value '0.203' for binary engine. Valid: ['off','on']`. That single line is why
  the sources bypassed the bus API and hand-rolled raw paho ([DEC-BUS-ENVELOPE-RAW-PAHO], 2026-07-19).
- change: "The create-on-demand path (`ComposeIOBus.ensure_namespace`) now defaults to the `json`
  engine, and validation is OPT-IN via explicit declaration (commit `de04af3`, 2026-08-08). Explicit
  `register_namespace()` keeps its own `binary` default, and every already-DECLARED namespace is
  unaffected — verified by test. The bypass therefore has no remaining justification."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "A namespace nobody declared was auto-created BINARY, so the first float/dict/string
    publish raised. 17 of 18 sources routed around the bus entirely rather than declare 30+ namespaces."
  - reasoning: "Auto-create fires exactly when nobody stated an intent to enforce; a 2-value engine is
    the wrong guess for an undeclared namespace in every case, not merely an unlucky one. The principle:
    validation is opt-in via DECLARATION."
  - rejected_alternative: "Infer the engine from the first value — rejected: `ContinuousEngine` clamps
    to 0-255, so a namespace whose first value was `42` would silently start REJECTING a later token
    count of `475000`. That converts a loud startup error into an unpredictable mid-run one."
  - counter_argument: "Raw paho works today, is proven on the glass, and has no state engine in the
    path at all — converting risks the console going dark for zero user-visible gain."
  - counter_response: "The gain is not user-visible and is not meant to be: it is substrate
    independence ([CXC-SOB-THESIS]) plus the removal of a per-source reconnect-storm surface. The
    dark-risk is real and is answered structurally by [CXC-SOB-TWO-STAGE] (route at unchanged scope
    first), the capture-diff bar (SOB-C2), and the slice order (SOB [CXC-SOB-SLICES])."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That `ensure_namespace`'s auto-create default stays permissive (`json`)
    and that DECLARED namespaces keep their declared engine. If auto-create is ever tightened back
    toward a validating default, every source that relies on declare-then-publish must be re-audited —
    and the bypass argument becomes live again."
  - implication: "A source may now publish a float, a dict, or a multi-kilobyte list through
    `bus.publish_state` without declaring anything. It MUST still declare — see [CXC-SOB-DECLARE] —
    but for SCOPE, not for the engine."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

---

## 3. The mechanism — how a source reaches the bus

### [CXC-SOB-HANDLE] The bus handle is INJECTED in-process and self-created standalone

- requirement: every converted source's `run(...)` and `main(argv, ...)` gain exactly two optional
  keyword parameters, with these EXACT names and defaults:
  - `bus: ComposeIOBus | None = None` — the bus to publish/subscribe on.
  - `stop: threading.Event | None = None` — the parking signal ([CXC-SOB-RUN-BLOCKS]).
  Positional `argv` stays first and unchanged. No other signature change is permitted, because
  `DataSourceSupervisor` binds `functools.partial(main, argv, ...)` and the standalone `__main__` path
  must keep passing argv alone.
- requirement: `bus=None` MUST self-create, never fail. The self-created bus is built through the
  substrate registry, NOT by constructing `MQTTTransport` directly:
  `ComposeIOBus(bus_id=<source-label>, global_transport=composeio.transport_registry.build(spec, app_id=<source-label>))`,
  where `spec` defaults to `{"type": "mqtt", "broker": <host from --mqtt>, "port": <port>}`. Going
  through the registry is what makes a standalone source substrate-independent too; hardcoding
  `MQTTTransport` here would reintroduce [CXC-SOB-THESIS]'s defect one level down.
- requirement: the wiring is ONE seam, not 20 copies. A shared helper module
  `python/composeio/source_bus.py` exposes `acquire_bus(...)`, `declare(...)` ([CXC-SOB-DECLARE]) and
  `park(...)` ([CXC-SOB-RUN-BLOCKS]); every source calls it BY REFERENCE. A per-source copy of this
  logic is the fork-divergence class ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]) and is a review reject.
- requirement: `intelligence/console/data_sources.py` `build_console_sources(...)` gains a
  `bus: ComposeIOBus | None = None` parameter and threads it into every `_src(...)` partial;
  `start_console_data_layer(...)` gains the same and `runtime._maybe_start_inprocess_sources` passes
  `app.bus`. When `bus is None` at the supervisor level (a headless `python3 data_sources.py` run),
  each source self-creates as above — behaviour identical to today except for the transport class.
- default: `bus_id` for a self-created bus is the source's existing paho client-id string
  (e.g. `lts-engine-status`), so broker-side identification is unchanged.
- verify-machine: `test_source_bus_seam.py` — (a) `main(argv)` with no kwargs self-creates a bus whose
  `global_transport` came from the registry (assert via an injected fake factory, no network);
  (b) `main(argv, bus=fake)` publishes ONLY on `fake` and constructs no transport;
  (c) `build_console_sources(bus=fake)` binds the SAME `fake` into all N partials.
- relates-to: [CXC-SOB-STANDALONE], [DD-SOB-HANDLE-SEAM]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### A publish made before the injected bus's transport is started is HELD and sent on first connect, never dropped [CXC-SOB-PRE-START-HOLD]

- requirement: an in-process source publishes on the INJECTED `app.bus` ([CXC-SOB-HANDLE]), and `runtime.main()` starts the sources (`_maybe_start_inprocess_sources`) BEFORE `app.run()` reaches `RenderApp.start()` and so `self.bus.start()`. Every source publishes NOW, then parks, so each source's FIRST value is sent to a global transport that is not on the wire yet. A global transport MUST treat "not started" exactly like "started, not yet connected": the message is HELD in the same bounded, order-preserving queue and flushed on the first successful connect. It MUST NOT be dropped.
- requirement: the hold is the SAME queue as the pre-connect hold (`MQTTTransport._pending`, cap `PENDING_MAX`, oldest dropped and COUNTED, reported on flush and in `health()["pending"]`). A second queue for the pre-start case is the fork-divergence class ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]) and is a review reject.
- requirement: the queue is appended from every source thread and flushed on the transport's network thread, so "check the link, then hold" in `send()` and "mark connected, then flush" in the connect callback MUST each run under ONE lock. Without it a send can read "not connected", lose the CPU, and append to the fresh queue after the flush swapped the old one out: that message is then held while the link is up, and the sender's next message overtakes it. Under concurrent senders and flushes no message is lost or duplicated, and each thread's messages reach the wire in the order that thread sent them ([CXC-SOB-THREAD-SAFETY]).
- requirement: a change-only source (`fleet_roster` publishes only `if force or current != last`) records a value as published when `publish_state` returns. The hold is what makes that record TRUE: the forced first roster MUST reach the wire on the first connect, with no content change in between.
- constraint: the "never started is a configuration error, queueing would hide it" premise (the MQTT pre-connect queue of 2026-07-21) is WITHDRAWN. Injection made pre-start publishing the normal path, not an error. A transport that is never started stays visible through `health()` (`started: False`, `pending: N`), the queue cap and the counted overflow, not by discarding data.
- rationale: the local bus already holds the value (`publish_state` stores it and notifies in-process subscribers whether or not any transport has started), so the console's own widgets are unaffected. What a drop loses is the WIRE copy: peers, and the broker's retained copy, see the PREVIOUS process's value (or nothing on a first boot) until the source's next tick, and a change-only source does not re-send until its content changes. That is a wire delta NOT on [CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS]'s list, and that node says such a delta is a DEFECT.
- evidence: measured 2026-11-27 on a console restart at 825ae016: 20 lines `MQTT transport not started, dropping message to '<ns>'`, all STATE, all during startup; the previous run (older code) logged the same 20. `send()` is byte-identical from the public beta (d856590) through 825ae016.
- verify-machine: `python/tests/test_transport_mqtt_pending.py`: `::test_send_before_start_is_held_then_flushed_on_first_connect`, `::test_pre_start_hold_is_the_same_bounded_queue`, `::test_bus_publish_before_start_reaches_the_wire` (the console ordering: `bus.publish_state` before `bus.start()`, then a connect; the value MUST be on the fake broker's wire), `::test_a_send_racing_the_connect_is_never_stranded_or_reordered` (deterministic: the race window is forced open), `::test_concurrent_senders_and_flushes_lose_nothing_and_keep_per_thread_order` (stress, fixed seeds), `::test_fleet_roster_first_publish_reaches_the_wire_after_connect`.
- verify-machine-result: EMPTY
- verify-human: EMPTY (no glass component; a peer-visible check is `mosquitto_sub -t 'composeio/global/fleet/roster' -C 1` within one second of a console restart)
- relates-to: [CXC-SOB-HANDLE], [CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS], [CXC-SOB-RUN-BLOCKS], [CXC-SOB-THREAD-SAFETY]
- see-also: [CXC-SOB-THREAD-SAFETY], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-STANDALONE] Standalone runnability is a first-class acceptance criterion, not a side effect

- requirement: for EVERY converted source, `python3 <module>.py <the exact argv data_sources.py passes>`
  MUST produce the same wire traffic as the in-process thread. `--selftest` (where a module has one)
  MUST stay green and MUST NOT require a broker.
- rationale: [DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES] made the sources threads; it explicitly did NOT make them
  thread-only. The standalone path is the debugging surface and the headless fallback
  (`COMPOSEIO_INPROCESS_SOURCES=0`), and it is the only way to bisect a source in isolation when the
  console goes dark.
- verify-machine: SOB-C6 — a test that runs each converted module's `--selftest` in a subprocess with
  no broker reachable and asserts exit 0.
- verify-human: for the slice containing `channel_bridge`, David runs it standalone against the live
  broker and confirms a round trip before the in-process conversion lands ([CXC-SOB-CHANNEL-LAST]).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-DECLARE] Scope is declared EXPLICITLY at the top of `run()`; auto-resolve is forbidden

- requirement: before its first publish, every converted source MUST declare each namespace it will
  publish, with an EXPLICIT scope, through the one helper:
  `source_bus.declare(bus, ns, scope=Scope.GLOBAL, engine="json", label=...)`. `Scope.AUTO` is
  FORBIDDEN for any namespace in [CXC-SOB-INVENTORY]. Declaration is co-located with the publisher —
  there is deliberately NO central scope table module ([CXC-SOB-NO-POLICY-LAYER]); the inventory in
  this spec is the audit reference, not a runtime artifact.
- requirement (conflict is LOUD): `bus.ensure_namespace` only upgrades an `AUTO` namespace and
  otherwise leaves an existing explicit scope untouched — so a scene-declared `scope: global` in
  `console.compose.yaml` would SILENTLY win over a source's `Scope.LOCAL`. `source_bus.declare` MUST
  therefore read `bus.get_namespace(ns)` first and, if an explicit scope already exists that DIFFERS
  from the requested one, RAISE at startup with both values named. A scope disagreement between the
  scene and the source is a real defect; resolving it silently is the failure class this whole arc is
  removing.
- requirement (the startup manifest): each source MUST emit, once, at startup, a single stderr line
  naming every namespace it declared with its resolved scope, e.g.
  `[lts_engine_status] declared: self/lts_engine/status scope=global`. This is acceptance criterion
  SOB-C3's mechanism: "no namespace silently goes dark" is checkable by reading one boot log.
- rationale: `_resolve_scope` falls back to "local if local subscribers exist, global otherwise". That
  heuristic already caused a real defect — [DEC-COMPOSER-SEND-GLOBAL-SCOPE], where a composer
  self-subscribed its own outbound namespace, auto-resolved to LOCAL, and the message died in-process
  LOOKING SENT. The console is full of self-subscribing widgets; leaving scope to the heuristic here
  would reproduce that defect across 30+ namespaces at once.
- verify-machine: `test_sources_declare_explicit_scope.py` — drive every source in
  `build_console_sources(bus=recording_bus)` and assert (a) every namespace it publishes was declared
  BEFORE the first publish, and (b) no declared namespace resolves through `Scope.AUTO`. A namespace
  published without a prior explicit declaration FAILS. This is the class guard; it needs no table and
  cannot drift from the code.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-NO-POLICY-LAYER] `scope` IS the uplink control — do not build a second one

- constraint: `bus._send_message` ALWAYS delivers locally and forwards to the global transport ONLY
  when scope is `global`/`auto`. A namespace registered `Scope.LOCAL` never reaches MQTT. This was
  proven directly on 2026-08-08. Selective uplink shipped long ago and is called `scope`.
- constraint: an earlier attempt at a separate `uplink_policy` module (plus 45 tests) was RETRACTED for
  exactly this reason ([DEC-RETRACT-UPLINK-POLICY-AND-DELETE-BROWSER-CONSOLE-ASSET]) — a parallel
  mechanism for something the bus already does, i.e. the fork-divergence class, committed inside the
  session spent fixing it. This spec MUST NOT reintroduce it under any name: no allow-list module, no
  policy table, no `uplink.yaml`. If a future need genuinely cannot be expressed as a namespace's
  `scope`, that is a bus-protocol change and belongs in [plt.cio:SPEC:CIO-BUS-PROTOCOL], not here.
- why-it-looked-real: the sources BYPASS the bus, so `scope` is inert for them — nothing consults it
  because nothing goes through `publish_state`. The symptom ("everything uplinks unconditionally") was
  genuine; the cause was a BYPASSED policy, not a MISSING one. This spec fixes the bypass, which is
  what makes `scope` live.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS] The four wire deltas that ARE permitted, enumerated so the capture-diff can normalize

- definition: routing through the bus legitimately changes four things. Anything NOT on this list that
  the capture-diff surfaces is a DEFECT, not an expected delta. The diff tool MUST normalize exactly
  these fields and no others.
  1. **`seq`** — today each source keeps its OWN per-source counter; the bus assigns one
     process-global monotonic `_next_seq()` (uint16 rollover) across all namespaces. Values change;
     monotonicity per-namespace is NOT preserved. `BusMessage.from_json` defaults `seq` to 0 and no
     console consumer reads it. **Normalize: drop `seq` from the diff.** See [DD-SOB-SEQ-SEMANTICS].
  2. **`ts`** — wall clock; already differs run to run. **Normalize: drop `ts`.**
  3. **`qos`** — `_QOS_MAP[STATE] = 1`, so sources currently publishing at qos 0
     (`lts_engine_status`; the `_app/console/bbox` reads) move 0 -> 1. qos is a delivery guarantee, not
     payload; an upgrade cannot lose a message. **Normalize: qos is out of the payload diff; record it
     separately and assert it only ever increases.**
  4. **Self-echo** — a source's own `global` publish returns to the same process via the transport's
     incoming dispatch. `_dispatch_incoming` calls `ns.publish_state` (NOT `bus.publish_state`), so
     there is no re-send and no loop; and `Namespace.publish_state` notifies local subscribers only
     when `new != old`, so an echoed identical value is absorbed. **Requirement: every converted
     source publishes `MessageType.STATE`. A source that needs EVENT semantics must handle its own
     echo explicitly and say so in its module docstring** — an EVENT echo is NOT deduplicated and would
     double-fire every in-process handler.
- constraint: `src` MUST be preserved byte-for-byte. `bus.publish_state(ns, value, source=...)` — the
  `source=` argument MUST be the exact string the source stamps today (`"self"`, `"transcript"`,
  `"deliverable-monitor"`, `"lts_engine_status"`, ...). A changed `src` is a wire change, and the
  chat/thread widgets key voices off it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-THREAD-SAFETY] One bus, 20 publisher threads — prove it before the fan-out lands

- requirement: the console bus is published to concurrently from ~20 daemon threads. Before slice 2
  lands, a stress test MUST prove the seam is safe: N=20 threads x M=500 `bus.publish_state` calls each
  against a recording transport; assert (a) no exception, (b) exactly N*M messages reached the
  transport, (c) no interleaved-envelope corruption (each captured payload parses and its `ns` matches
  the publishing thread's namespace).
- known-hazard: `ComposeIOBus._next_seq` is not locked, and `_message_log` is rebound by slicing under
  concurrent append. Neither is fatal (`seq` is advisory per [CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS]; a lost log entry is
  cosmetic), but both MUST be confirmed non-fatal by the test rather than assumed. If the test finds a
  real corruption, the fix belongs in `bus.py` under a lock — NOT in a per-source workaround.
- rationale: paho `publish()` from multiple threads against one `loop_start()` client is documented
  thread-safe; the strategy that motivated this work explicitly said it should be PROVEN under the real
  fan-out rather than trusted from the doc.
- verify-machine: `python/tests/test_bus_concurrent_publish.py`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]
- see-also: [CXC-SOB-PRE-START-HOLD] (the transport's pre-connect queue is the other shared structure on this fan-out: appended from every source thread, flushed on the network thread, and locked)

### [CXC-SOB-LOG-NOISE] The 256-byte guideline must not turn the console log into a warning storm

- known-hazard: `MQTTTransport._send_now` logs a WARNING for any payload over 256 bytes (DP-CIO-18).
  The console's payloads are routinely KILOBYTES — transcript narration, the deliverable ledger, the
  agents/skills arrays, the defect roll-up, the projects report. Routing 20 sources through the bus
  will emit hundreds of such warnings per minute where today there are none.
- requirement: the size check is THREE-TIERED on `MQTTTransport._send_now`, and each tier is fixed, not configurable. (a) at or below `GUIDELINE_PAYLOAD_BYTES` (256): SILENT. (b) above it and at or below `LARGE_PAYLOAD_WARN_BYTES`: `logger.debug`, naming DP-CIO-18 — the console's normal case. (c) above `LARGE_PAYLOAD_WARN_BYTES`: `logger.warning`, ONCE PER NAMESPACE PER PROCESS, never once per send. Both thresholds are CLASS CONSTANTS on `MQTTTransport`, not per-call arguments and not runtime-configurable — an earlier draft of this clause said "configurable ... (default: ...)" while its own next sentence said "module constant", and the code implemented the constant; the word `configurable` was the error. `LARGE_PAYLOAD_WARN_BYTES` MUST sit above every payload in [CXC-SOB-INVENTORY], MEASURED from the real projection function, never estimated from a multiple of one sampled source. Amended by [CXC-SOB-LOG-NOISE-CALIBRATION].
- rationale: a warning that fires hundreds of times a minute is a warning nobody reads, and it will
  bury the LOUD failures ([CXC-SOB-DECLARE] conflicts, transport-unavailable degradation) that this
  arc depends on being visible. Preserving a guideline by making the log useless is a net loss. This
  clause is not a prediction — it is what HAPPENED, to this very change: see
  [CXC-SOB-LOG-NOISE-CALIBRATION].
- verify-machine: `python/tests/render/test_mqtt_log_noise.py`. The probes MUST include the REAL payload, built by calling `intelligence/publisher/deliverable_publisher.compute_payload` over the checked-in ~180-node fixture `python/tests/render/fixtures/deliverable_ledger_178.lts` and asserting ZERO WARNING records; plus N oversize sends on ONE namespace asserting EXACTLY 1 warning; plus two distinct oversize namespaces asserting 2. The superseded version prescribed 4 KiB and 128 KiB synthetic probes — neither is a real console payload, and every actual one (16 KB to 110 KB) fell in the untested gap between them, which is how this suite stayed green while defending a live defect. A synthetic probe MAY bracket a tier; it may never be the ONLY evidence for a threshold.
- verify-human: `grep -c "runaway threshold" console.log` against `wc -l console.log` on a multi-hour session. The clause is a RATIO, so the check is a ratio: runaway warnings must be a rounding error in the log, not a share of it. The measurement that opened this amendment was 27,009 in 58,589 lines (46%); the passing shape is single digits. No machine test can assert this — it needs a real log from a real session — which is exactly why it is stated separately from `verify-machine` rather than assumed to fall out of it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-LOG-NOISE-CALIBRATION] The runaway threshold shipped ALREADY-VIOLATED — raise it AND report once per namespace

- change: `MQTTTransport.LARGE_PAYLOAD_WARN_BYTES` 64 KiB -> 256 KiB, AND the WARNING is now emitted ONCE PER NAMESPACE PER PROCESS (`MQTTTransport._large_payload_warned`, a set of namespaces already reported). Both halves, not either. The warning text also drops the clause "this is very likely a bug, not a large payload".
- context_for_change: [CXC-SOB-LOG-NOISE] was authored to PREVENT a warning storm and PRODUCED one, on the day it shipped. 64 KiB was calibrated to `self/projects` (16,465 B measured, x4 ~= 65,536) — while this node's own `known-hazard` list names the deliverable ledger FIRST. On the authoring commit `9c29e3c` the `self/deliverables` payload was already 70,333 bytes on the wire: over on day one. Five days later the live console log carried 27,009 runaway warnings in 58,589 lines — 46% of the file — which is verbatim the failure its `rationale` clause forbids.
- invalidating_assumption: that ONE sampled payload times a round multiple bounds the whole enumerated set. It does not, and the sample was not even the largest member — the spec's own hazard list named a bigger one two lines above the number. Calibrating a threshold against a sample instead of against the ENUMERATION is the defect; the arithmetic was never the problem.
- measurement: `self/deliverables` wire size, measured through `deliverable_publisher.compute_payload` + `BusMessage.to_json`: 70,333 B at authoring (104 ledger nodes) -> ~110,100 B on 2026-08-13 (178 nodes). The payload is CORRECT and fully BOUNDED at both points — 178 rows <-> 178 ledger nodes, zero duplicates, every declared cap functioning. The THRESHOLD was the defect, never the payload.
- decision: raise AND deduplicate. Raising alone only resets the clock: `all` is an unbounded projection of a corpus that grew 104 -> 178 nodes in five days, so any absolute value is a countdown. Once-per-namespace is the structural half — it makes the absolute value NON-CRITICAL, because a re-crossing costs ONE line instead of a storm.
- alternatives: (a) raise only — REJECTED, resets the clock without removing the failure mode. (b) a growth-RATE or relative detector — REJECTED: it needs a rate and a window, both calibrated against payloads its author has in hand, which is the identical disease one level up. Prefer the change that REMOVES the failure mode over the change that makes the detector cleverer. (c) suppress the warning for named namespaces — REJECTED, an allowlist is a per-call opt-out wearing a hat, and [DX-NARROW-WAIST] forbids it.
- correction: this node's superseded text claimed the guideline was "still LOGGED at DEBUG, where a substrate-portability audit can find every oversized payload". MEASURED FALSE: `grep -c DEBUG console.log` = 0 across 58,589 lines / ~30 h, against a working control. The DEBUG tier is not enabled and never has been, so it has written NOTHING and the audit it promised has never been possible. The claim is RETRACTED rather than the tier enabled — see `key-insight`.
- key-insight: once the warning is once-per-namespace, the WARNING LINE IS THE AUDIT RECORD: exactly one line per oversized namespace per process, at a level that is actually enabled. The capability the DEBUG tier was claimed to provide is delivered by the deduplication instead — which is why enabling DEBUG (thousands of lines of the same noise one level quieter) would have been the wrong repair.
- consequence: the test suite was GREEN while actively defending this defect: `test_mqtt_log_noise.py` asserted `LARGE_PAYLOAD_WARN_BYTES == 64 * 1024` and probed the tiers at 4 KiB and 128 KiB — neither a real console payload, with every actual one (16 KB to 110 KB) in the untested gap between them. A magic-number pin can be satisfied without a payload ever being measured. It is replaced by the INVARIANT (`threshold > the largest enumerated payload, measured`) and by a regression built from the real `compute_payload` over a checked-in fixture.
- out: `deliverable_publisher` republishing the ENTIRE ledger every 4 s regardless of change (~2.9 GB per 30 h session at QoS 1). Real, separately filed, and deliberately NOT fixed here: folding it in would produce exactly the over-broad root cause this diagnosis avoided. It is a bandwidth defect; this one is a calibration defect, and they share only a namespace.
- status: operational
- decided-in-session: 2026-08-13
- see-also: [CXC-SOB-LOG-NOISE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

---

## 4. The classification — which namespaces are external

### [CXC-SOB-EXTERNALITY] Externality is a fact about CONSUMER LOCALITY, not about the namespace's name

- definition: a namespace is EXTERNAL if any of its publishers OR consumers lives in a different OS
  process from the console. It is INTRA-APP only if EVERY publisher and EVERY consumer is inside the
  console process. The test is a census of running processes, never an intuition about the prefix.
- constraint: a namespace family is classified WHOLE, never by a narrower glob. An earlier pass used
  `_app/*/open` and missed `_app/dsd/answered` and `_app/decisions/anchor`, which are published BY
  spawned viewer windows. A per-leaf classification is one forgotten leaf away from a dark surface;
  declare the family.
- constraint: a namespace whose locality is UNPROVEN is EXTERNAL. Default-deny is the quiet-bus stance,
  but "forgotten goes dark" is this system's worst failure mode and its failures are silent. Uplinking
  a namespace nobody consumes costs bandwidth; withholding one somebody does costs a dark surface with
  no error. The asymmetry decides it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-FUN-IS-EXTERNAL] `fun/nightwatch/*` is EXTERNAL — it feeds a SPAWNED WINDOW, not an in-process widget

- correction: the working classification carried into this spec listed `fun/*` as intra-app. It is not.
  `intelligence/tools/away_watch_publisher.py` publishes `fun/nightwatch/{title,started,ended,decisions,
  tokens,elapsed,gitstate,tasks,artifacts,tab,greeting,message,sessions}` AND spawns
  `intelligence/console/nightwatch.compose.yaml` as a SEPARATE process
  (`subprocess.Popen([... nightwatch.compose.yaml, "--transports"])`). That window's `RenderApp` builds
  its OWN bus and receives those namespaces over MQTT. Declaring `fun/*` local would black out the
  entire Away Watch window with no error — the exact silent-dark failure this arc exists to remove.
- evidence: `away_watch_publisher.py` (`_spawn`/`relaunch`, scene path
  `intelligence/console/nightwatch.compose.yaml`) and `nightwatch.compose.yaml` `namespaces:` block.
- implication: `fun/nightwatch/*` is EXTERNAL by exactly the same construction as `_app/*` — a spawned
  viewer window is a separate OS process. It is `scope: global` in Stage A AND in Stage B.
- generalization: this is the second time a "spawned window" consumer was missed by a name-based
  classification. [CXC-SOB-EXTERNALITY]'s family rule and [CXC-SOB-CENSUS]'s process census exist to
  make the third time impossible.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-INVENTORY] The per-source namespace and scope inventory

- definition: the authoritative enumeration for acceptance criterion SOB-C3. The SOURCE COUNT is
  whatever `build_console_sources` returns (20 with a pinned session, 17 without — asserted by
  `data_sources._selftest` check 17); this table enumerates by NAME so a newly registered source that
  is missing here is a review reject rather than a silent gap.
- reading the table: **Stage A** is the scope declared when the source is routed through the bus, chosen
  to keep the wire IDENTICAL ([CXC-SOB-TWO-STAGE]). **Stage B** is the candidate scope after a passed
  census ([CXC-SOB-CENSUS]) — a candidate, never an authorization.

| # | Source | Direction | Namespaces | Consumers outside the process? | Stage A | Stage B candidate |
|---|--------|-----------|------------|--------------------------------|---------|-------------------|
| 1 | `self_publisher` | pub | `self/<id>/*` (context_level, context_pct, registered, rate_limit, ...) | UNPROVEN — `host/poc_driver` subscribes `composeio/global/self/#`; a fleet view subscribes `self/+/context_pct` | global | census-gated |
| 2 | `transcript_publisher` | pub | `thread/<id>/narration`, `self/<id>/activity`, `self/<id>/subagents`, `self/<id>/avatar/expression` | UNPROVEN — same fleet/host surface | global | census-gated |
| 3 | `deliverable_monitor` | pub+sub | `deliverable/new`, `deliverable/modified` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 4 | `channel_bridge` | pub+sub | `intelligence/<id>/{in,out,channel/alive}`, `intelligence/<id>/compose` | YES — the MCP channel server (`channel.mjs`), the launcher harness, David | global | **global (never local)** |
| 5 | `deliverable_publisher` | pub | `self/deliverables` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 6 | `deliverable_opener` | sub | `_app/deliverable/open`, `_app/fs/open` | YES — `_app/*` family | global | global |
| 7 | `deliverable_watcher` | pub | `deliverable/new`, `deliverable/modified` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 8 | `memory_monitor` | pub | `self/memory` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 9 | `away_watch_publisher` | pub+sub | `self/away_watches`, `fun/nightwatch/*`, `_app/away_watch/open`, `_app/console/bbox` | YES — the spawned Night Watch window ([CXC-SOB-FUN-IS-EXTERNAL]) | global | **global** |
| 10 | `defect_metrics_publisher` | pub | `self/<id>/defects` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 11 | `project_validator` | pub | `self/projects` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 12 | `doc_viewer_launcher` | sub | `_app/doc/open`, `_app/console/bbox` | YES — `_app/*` family | global | global |
| 13 | `decisions_viewer_launcher` | sub | `_app/graph/open`, `_app/decisions/anchor`, `_app/console/bbox` | YES — published BY spawned windows | global | global |
| 14 | `dsd_viewer_launcher` | sub | `_app/dsd/open`, `_app/dsd/answered`, `_app/console/bbox` | YES — `_app/dsd/answered` is published BY the spawned DSD window | global | global |
| 15 | `lts_engine_status` | pub | `self/lts_engine/status` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 16 | `config_persister` | pub+sub | `intelligence/<id>/config/{retention,heartbeat}`, `intelligence/<id>/config/console/*` | YES — originates outside the process (the channel server, the launcher harness, David) | global | **global** |
| 17 | `agents_skills_publisher` | pub | `self/<id>/agents`, `self/<id>/skills` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 18 | `memory_publisher` | pub | `self/<id>/memories` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 19 | `meta_publisher` | pub | `self/meta` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |
| 20 | `daemon_watch` | pub | `self/daemons` | UNPROVEN | global | census-gated |

- family rules, stated once so a new leaf inherits them:
  - `_app/*` — EXTERNAL, WHOLE FAMILY, permanently `global`. Cross-process by construction: the
    standalone `app_launcher`, the standalone `decisions_curator`, `console_supervisor`
    (`_app/console/<id>/{lifecycle,state}`), and every spawned viewer window both publish and consume
    here.
  - `intelligence/<id>/*` — EXTERNAL, permanently `global`. It originates outside the process.
  - `fun/*` — EXTERNAL, permanently `global` ([CXC-SOB-FUN-IS-EXTERNAL]).
  - `self/*`, `thread/*`, `deliverable/*` — intra-app CANDIDATES. Census-gated, not assumed.
- verify-machine: SOB-C3 — a test asserts every namespace observed on a full `build_console_sources`
  drive appears in this table's name set (fixture-mirrored), and vice versa. A source added without a
  table row is a red test.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

