## 5. The hazard — a converted source must still BLOCK

### [CXC-SOB-RUN-BLOCKS] A converted source's `run()` MUST park for the lifetime of the source

- invariant: `run()` MUST NOT return while the source is meant to be alive. Two sources —
  `intelligence/publisher/channel_bridge.py` and `intelligence/publisher/deliverable_opener.py` — call
  `client.loop_forever()`, and that blocking call is HOW THEIR SOURCE THREAD STAYS ALIVE. Once they
  publish/subscribe through the bus there is no `loop_forever` to block on, and a source that simply
  returns is MIS-READ.
- why it is mis-read: `DataSourceSupervisor._guarded` treats a CLEAN RETURN from `run()` as "this source
  yielded its host singleton" and RE-INVOKES it after `reclaim_poll_s` — the reclaim-on-release behaviour
  from [CXC-IC-SINGLETON] / IC-C27, generalized to the source class after
  [DEFECT-IN-PROCESS-SINGLETON-SOURCE-YIELDS]. A converted source that returns therefore enters a
  RECLAIM RETRY LOOP that re-subscribes every 5 seconds forever, and reports NO error. The failure is a
  slow leak of duplicate routes plus a log the operator learns to ignore — not a crash.
- requirement: every converted source parks through the ONE helper,
  `source_bus.park(stop, poll_s=...)`, which blocks on the injected `stop` Event (creating a private one
  when `stop is None`, the standalone case) and returns ONLY when it is set. `loop_forever()` and
  `loop_start()` are both banned by the [CXC-SOB-THESIS] grep guard; the shared client already runs its
  own loop and a second one conflicts.
- requirement: `DataSourceSupervisor` owns the stop events. `DataSource` gains
  `stop: threading.Event | None`; `build_console_sources` creates one per source and binds it into the
  partial; `DataSourceSupervisor.stop()` sets EVERY source's event BEFORE joining, so `stop()` joins the
  real sources cleanly instead of relying on daemon-thread death. This makes the existing "test doubles
  that honour a stop signal join cleanly" path the REAL path.
- verify-machine (this is the enforcing test the invariant requires, and it closes the CLASS, not the
  instance): `python/tests/render/test_sources_run_blocks.py` parametrizes over EVERY source returned by
  `build_console_sources(bus=<fake>, ...)` and, for each:
  1. runs `run()` on a thread with `stop` unset; asserts the thread is STILL ALIVE after 250 ms;
  2. asserts the supervisor logged NO `yielded its singleton` line in that window;
  3. sets `stop`; asserts the thread joins within 2 s.
  A source that returns early fails leg 1; a source that ignores `stop` fails leg 3. Both legs are
  required — a source that blocks forever and cannot be stopped is the other half of the same defect.
- constraint: the singleton `flock` YIELD path is the ONE legitimate early return and MUST be preserved
  — `acquire_singleton` returning None still returns from `run()` so the supervisor's reclaim loop can
  do its job. The test therefore drives sources with the singleton ACQUIRED (a temp lock dir), so leg 1
  measures the parking behaviour and not the yield behaviour.
- relates-to: [CXC-SOB-SUBSCRIBE], [CXC-IC-SINGLETON]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-SUBSCRIBE] Subscribing through the bus — pattern vs exact, and the callback contract

- requirement: a source that today calls `client.subscribe("composeio/+/<ns>")` converts to
  `bus.subscribe(ns, cb)`; one that subscribes an MQTT wildcard (`composeio/global/_app/+/open`)
  converts to `bus.subscribe_pattern("_app/*", cb)` and filters in the callback. The bus's own
  `MQTTTransport.subscribe` already issues `composeio/+/<ns>`, so scope-agnostic reception is preserved
  without the source naming a topic.
- requirement: the bus callback signature is `(namespace, new_value, old_value)` for
  `bus.subscribe` — NOT paho's `(client, userdata, msg)`, and NOT a `BusMessage`. Every converted
  handler MUST be rewritten to that signature and MUST NOT parse JSON: the value arrives decoded. A
  handler that still calls `json.loads(msg.payload)` will raise on every message.
- known-hazard: `Namespace.publish_state` notifies subscribers ONLY when `new != old`. A source that
  needs to act on every REPEAT of an identical value (an idempotent "open this path again" intent) will
  MISS the repeat. Two `_app/*` intents are of this shape (`_app/doc/open`, `_app/deliverable/open`:
  tapping the same row twice). Each converted subscriber MUST state in its docstring whether it needs
  repeat delivery; if it does, its publisher side MUST carry a nonce/timestamp in the payload so
  consecutive values differ. This is a REAL behaviour change and is the most likely source of a
  "sometimes the window doesn't open" report.
- verify-machine: a per-subscriber test publishing the SAME value twice and asserting the expected
  handler-invocation count (1 for dedup-tolerant, 2 for repeat-required after the nonce fix).
- verify-human: David taps the same deliverable row twice in a row on the glass and the viewer opens
  both times.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

---

## 6. Sequencing

### [CXC-SOB-TWO-STAGE] Route first at UNCHANGED scope; flip scope only after a census

- requirement: the work is TWO stages and they MUST NOT be bundled.
  - **Stage A — routing.** Every source publishes/subscribes through `ComposeIOBus`, every namespace
    declared `Scope.GLOBAL` (its CURRENT effective scope). The wire is byte-identical modulo
    [CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS]. The capture-diff must be CLEAN. This stage delivers the whole of
    [CXC-SOB-THESIS] and the whole connection collapse, and it can go dark in exactly one way (a source
    that fails to publish), which the diff catches.
  - **Stage B — selective uplink.** Census-gated per namespace family; flips a candidate to
    `Scope.LOCAL`. The capture-diff for a flipped namespace INTENTIONALLY shows it disappearing from the
    wire; the acceptance question becomes "did any consumer notice", which is a different, human
    question.
- rationale: the frozen-contract invariant ([CXC-SOB-CONTRACT-FROZEN]) and the selective-uplink goal
  ([DEC-INTERNAL-BUS-SELECTIVE-UPLINK]) are in direct tension: one says the wire must not change, the
  other's entire point is that intra-app traffic stops appearing on it. Splitting the stages resolves
  the tension honestly instead of letting a reviewer discover it mid-slice. It also keeps the rollback
  points meaningful: Stage A reverts as a transport change, Stage B as a one-line scope edit.
- constraint: Stage A is DONE and MEASURED before any Stage-B flip is proposed. Bundling a scope flip
  into a routing slice makes a dark surface indistinguishable from a routing bug.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-CENSUS] What a namespace family must pass before it may go LOCAL

- requirement: a family may flip to `Scope.LOCAL` only after ALL FOUR pass, recorded in the slice's PR:
  1. **Broker-side subscriber census.** With the console running and every spawned window open, capture
     `mosquitto_sub -h <broker> -t '$SYS/broker/subscriptions/#'` (or, where unavailable, the
     per-process `lsof`/connection inventory plus each peer's declared `namespaces:` block) and show no
     consumer outside the console process for the family.
  2. **Static reachability sweep.** Grep every `*.compose.yaml`, every `intelligence/**/*.py`, and every
     out-of-process peer in [CXC-SOB-EXEMPT] class 2 for the family; show every hit is in-process.
  3. **Dry-run soak.** Ship the family still `global`, with a startup line naming it as
     CANDIDATE-LOCAL, and run one full session (>= 8 h, including at least one `/bounce-console` and one
     spawned-window open of every kind). No new dark-surface report.
  4. **Named owner.** A human — David — signs off that no external consumer he knows of exists. The
     census can only prove what is running; the owner covers what is merely paused.
- constraint: the dry-run is a LOG LINE, not a shadow policy engine. It states intent and withholds
  nothing. Any implementation that builds a table of would-be-local namespaces is
  [CXC-SOB-NO-POLICY-LAYER]'s retracted `uplink_policy` wearing a hat.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-SLICES] The slice order — smallest risk first, `channel_bridge` last of all

- definition: each slice is independently landable, independently revertable, and ends with the
  acceptance run in [CXC-SOB-ACCEPT]. Slices 1-7 are Stage A; slice 8 is Stage B; slice 9 is the
  end-state fold.

| Slice | Content | Sources | Why here | Revert |
|-------|---------|---------|----------|--------|
| 1 | The seam only — `source_bus.py` (`acquire_bus` / `declare` / `park`), the `bus`/`stop` kwargs threaded through `data_sources.py`, the [CXC-SOB-THREAD-SAFETY] stress test, the [CXC-SOB-LOG-NOISE] demotion. ZERO sources converted. | none | Pure addition; no runtime behaviour changes at all. Everything after it is a small diff. | delete the module |
| 2 | The REFERENCE conversion — one source, end to end, with its capture-diff. | `lts_engine_status` | Single namespace, 10 s cadence, publish-only, no subscriber, and nothing catastrophic if it blips. It is also the qos 0 -> 1 case, so the delta is exercised on the least important namespace. | one file |
| 3 | Publish-only, session-INDEPENDENT sources, converted BY REFERENCE to slice 2. | `meta_publisher`, `memory_publisher`, `memory_monitor`, `agents_skills_publisher`, `deliverable_publisher`, `deliverable_watcher`, `defect_metrics_publisher`, `project_validator`, `daemon_watch` | Nine mechanical repetitions of a proven pattern; none is a subscriber; none needs the transcript. This is where the connection count drops hardest. | per file |
| 4 | Publish-only, TRANSCRIPT-based sources. | `self_publisher`, `transcript_publisher` | Highest volume and they drive the visible gauges, so they get their own slice and their own glass check — but they are still publish-only, which is the easy half. | per file |
| 5 | SUBSCRIBING sources that already use `loop_start` (they need the [CXC-SOB-SUBSCRIBE] callback rewrite but NOT the parking change). | `doc_viewer_launcher`, `decisions_viewer_launcher`, `dsd_viewer_launcher`, `away_watch_publisher`, `config_persister`, `deliverable_monitor` | First slice with the callback-signature and dedup hazards; sequenced after the publish-only pattern is proven so a failure here is unambiguously a subscribe failure. | per file |
| 6 | The FIRST `loop_forever` source. | `deliverable_opener` | Exercises [CXC-SOB-RUN-BLOCKS] for real on the lower-stakes of the two blocking sources. If parking is wrong, we learn it on "the file didn't open", not on "David can't talk to the Intelligence". | one file |
| 7 | The SECOND `loop_forever` source, ALONE. | `channel_bridge` | [CXC-SOB-CHANNEL-LAST]. | one file |
| 8 | Stage B — the census-gated scope flips, one FAMILY per commit. | (declarations only) | [CXC-SOB-TWO-STAGE], [CXC-SOB-CENSUS]. | one line per family |
| 9 | The end-state fold. | (module moves) | [CXC-SOB-ENDSTATE]. | git revert |

- constraint: a slice does not land red and does not land unmeasured. The connection count
  (SOB-C1) is recorded BEFORE slice 1 as the baseline and after EVERY slice; a slice that does not move
  it or that moves it UP is investigated before the next slice starts.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [CXC-SOB-CHANNEL-LAST] `channel_bridge` is converted last, alone, and verified independently first

- premise: `channel_bridge` is THE HUMAN'S CHANNEL to the Intelligence — it relays
  `intelligence/<id>/{in,out}` and unwraps the composer's `/compose`. If it breaks, David loses the
  ability to talk to the running Intelligence, and the failure mode is SILENCE, not an error. There is
  no gauge that goes grey; the messages simply stop arriving.
- requirement: its conversion is its OWN slice, its own commit, and its own revert point. It shares a
  slice with nothing.
- requirement (independent verification BEFORE the in-process conversion lands, in this order):
  1. The converted `channel_bridge` runs STANDALONE against the live broker
     (`python3 channel_bridge.py --id architect --mqtt mqtt://<broker>:1883`) with the in-process one
     DISABLED, and a round trip is confirmed: David sends a message from the composer, the Intelligence
     receives it once (not twice — the doubling defect [CXC-IC-SINGLETON] closed), the reply renders in
     the thread.
  2. A capture-diff on `intelligence/<id>/{in,out,channel/alive}` specifically, before and after.
  3. A fallback is REHEARSED, not assumed: confirm the revert command and confirm that reverting
     restores the channel within one console bounce.
  4. ONLY THEN does the in-process conversion land, followed immediately by a real bounce and a live
     round trip on the glass.
- verify-human: David personally confirms a send-and-reply round trip after the bounce. This one is NOT
  delegable to a machine signal: the failure mode is silence, and silence is exactly what a passing
  test looks like from the outside.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

---

## 7. Acceptance

### [CXC-SOB-ACCEPT] The acceptance criteria — machine signal AND, where the surface is human-perceptible, a glass gate

- definition: every slice in [CXC-SOB-SLICES] runs the criteria below that apply to it. Two-tier by
  construction: a machine signal is necessary and, for any surface a human perceives, never sufficient.

| ID | Criterion | when / then | Machine signal | Human gate |
|----|-----------|-------------|----------------|------------|
| SOB-C1 | Connection collapse | WHEN the console has been up 60 s THEN its TCP connections to the broker are at or below the previous slice's count, and after slice 7 EXACTLY 1 | `lsof -nP -p $(pgrep -f console.compose.yaml) \| grep -c ':1883'`, cross-checked against the peer address so a coincidental local port cannot inflate it. Baseline recorded BEFORE slice 1; terminal value MUST be 1 (the render runtime's one `MQTTTransport`). Secondary, recorded not gated: thread count via `ps -M`. | — |
| SOB-C2 | Wire compatibility by CAPTURE-DIFF | WHEN each namespace's traffic is captured off the broker for 120 s before and after a slice THEN the normalized payload sets are IDENTICAL | `mosquitto_sub -h <broker> -t 'composeio/#' -v` to a file, before and after; normalize by dropping `ts` and `seq` and sorting by `(topic, src, ns)` per [CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS]; `diff` is EMPTY. qos recorded separately and asserted non-decreasing. A namespace that appears in the BEFORE capture and not the AFTER is a slice blocker. | — |
| SOB-C3 | No namespace silently goes dark | WHEN the console starts THEN every namespace in [CXC-SOB-INVENTORY] is named in the boot log with its declared scope | the per-source startup manifest line ([CXC-SOB-DECLARE]) plus the inventory-parity test; a source with no manifest line fails | David greps one boot log and sees every expected namespace |
| SOB-C4 | Suites stay green | WHEN a slice lands THEN no test regresses | `pytest intelligence/` and the render suite BOTH green, with NO new failures AND no reduction in COLLECTED count except by a deletion explicitly recorded in the slice. Baselines at spec time: 173 and 2585 — recorded as the starting point, with the RULE (no unexplained drop) as the durable gate, because the counts grow every week. | — |
| SOB-C5 | `run()` still blocks | WHEN a converted source is started with `stop` unset THEN its thread is alive after 250 ms and the supervisor logs no reclaim | `test_sources_run_blocks.py`, all three legs, parametrized over every source ([CXC-SOB-RUN-BLOCKS]) | — |
| SOB-C6 | Standalone parity | WHEN a converted module is run as a script with the console's argv THEN it produces the same traffic; and its `--selftest` passes with no broker | subprocess test, exit 0, no network ([CXC-SOB-STANDALONE]) | — |
| SOB-C7 | No substrate names below the waist | WHEN the guard test runs THEN no registered source imports paho, builds a client, composes a `composeio/` topic, or calls `loop_*` | `test_sources_have_no_paho.py` with the [CXC-SOB-EXEMPT] allow-list | — |
| SOB-C8 | Concurrency is proven, not assumed | WHEN 20 threads publish 500 messages each THEN all 10 000 arrive intact | `test_bus_concurrent_publish.py` ([CXC-SOB-THREAD-SAFETY]) | — |
| SOB-C9 | Repeat-delivery hazard closed | WHEN an idempotent intent is published twice with the same value THEN its handler fires the documented number of times | per-subscriber dedup test ([CXC-SOB-SUBSCRIBE]) | David taps the same row twice; the window opens twice |
| SOB-C10 | The glass, after a REAL bounce | WHEN the console is bounced for real THEN every gauge is live | — (there is no machine signal for "the console looks right") | David runs `/bounce-console` and confirms live context gauge, live thread, live deliverables, live projects, live defects, and an open-then-close of every spawned window kind |
| SOB-C11 | The channel round trip | WHEN slice 7 has landed and the console bounced THEN David sends a message and gets a reply | the channel-bridge capture-diff | **David personally confirms send-and-reply.** Mandatory, non-delegable ([CXC-SOB-CHANNEL-LAST]) |
| SOB-C12 | Away Watch survives | WHEN the Night Watch window is opened after any slice THEN it renders live data, not its static defaults | `fun/nightwatch/*` present in the AFTER capture | David opens the Away Watch window and sees real values ([CXC-SOB-FUN-IS-EXTERNAL]) |

- constraint: SOB-C10 and SOB-C11 CANNOT be waived by a green machine run. The console's failure mode is
  that the UX renders perfectly while the data is stale or absent — a glass check is the only instrument
  that sees it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

---

## 8. The end state

### [CXC-SOB-ENDSTATE] Once the sources are on the bus, `bus_envelope` and `bus_client` become INTERNAL to `transport_mqtt`

- definition: `composeio/bus_envelope.py` (the one STATE-envelope framer) and `composeio/bus_client.py`
  (the shared paho connection + the topic-dispatch router) exist as APP-FACING helpers only because the
  app currently speaks MQTT. Once no source imports them, both are MQTT implementation detail and belong
  BEHIND the transport: moved to `composeio/transport_mqtt/` (or made private module members of it),
  with no importer outside that module.
- rationale: MQTT knowledge belongs in the MQTT transport. Leaving an app-facing `bus_client` in place
  after the conversion leaves a live, documented, tempting path back around the bus — and the next
  source someone adds will take it, exactly as 17 sources took the last one.
- requirement: this is a LATER SLICE (slice 9), not part of Stage A, and it is gated on the
  [CXC-SOB-THESIS] grep guard being green with an empty non-exempt set. Its acceptance is a pure import
  check: `grep -rn "bus_client\|bus_envelope" --include=*.py` returns hits ONLY inside
  `python/composeio/transport_mqtt*` and their own tests.
- constraint: `intelligence/launcher/launcher.py` keeps its hand-framing ([CXC-SOB-EXEMPT]); the fold
  MUST NOT create an import from the launcher into `composeio`, which is the exact footprint that
  exemption protects.
- relates-to: [CXC-SOB-THESIS]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

---

## 9. Open decisions — flagged for the architect's ratify, NOT resolved here

### [DD-SOB-HANDLE-SEAM] How a source obtains its bus: injected kwarg vs a process-wide accessor

- decision: PROPOSED — the injected `bus=None` kwarg on `run()`/`main(argv, ...)`, self-creating when
  absent ([CXC-SOB-HANDLE]). Specified normatively so the builder is not blocked; flagged here because
  it is a real fork in the road and the architect owns it.
- rationale: it matches the prior recommendation in the 2026-08-08 connection-collapse strategy
  ("add an optional injected bus/client that defaults to self-created"), it preserves the
  [DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES] standalone contract exactly, it keeps every dependency EXPLICIT and therefore
  testable with a fake, and it needs no process-global mutable state.
- alternatives: (a) a process-wide accessor `source_bus.current()` mirroring `bus_client.acquire()` —
  fewer signature changes, but it is ambient global state, it makes a test's bus leak between test
  cases, and it hides which sources touch the bus; (b) constructing the bus inside
  `build_console_sources` and passing it positionally — breaks argv parity and the standalone path.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify
- invalidating_assumption: "That a source needs at most ONE bus. If a source ever has to bridge TWO
  buses (e.g. an MQTT leg and a CAN leg simultaneously — the `can_mqtt_bridge` shape), a single `bus=`
  kwarg is the wrong signature and this reopens as `buses: Mapping[str, ComposeIOBus]`."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [DD-SOB-LOCAL-FLIP] Which namespace families actually flip to LOCAL in Stage B

- decision: DEFERRED to Stage B, per family, gated by [CXC-SOB-CENSUS]. This spec deliberately declares
  EVERY namespace `global` in Stage A and does NOT pick the Stage-B set.
- rationale: the classification carried into this spec was wrong about `fun/*` ([CXC-SOB-FUN-IS-EXTERNAL]),
  and `self/*` has at least two candidate out-of-process consumers (`host/poc_driver`'s
  `composeio/global/self/#` subscription and the fleet `self/+/context_pct` view described in
  `self_publisher`). A classification that has already been wrong once, about a family that would black
  out a whole window, has not earned a same-slice flip.
- alternatives: (a) flip `self/*` + `thread/*` + `deliverable/*` in Stage A on the strength of the
  existing classification — rejected: it bundles a design decision into a mechanical win and makes a
  dark surface indistinguishable from a routing bug; (b) never flip, keep everything global — rejected:
  it abandons [DEC-INTERNAL-BUS-SELECTIVE-UPLINK] and keeps the bus noisy, and the routing change alone
  does not deliver the decided end-state.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify
- invalidating_assumption: "That the Intelligence Host spawner keeps spawning builder consoles as
  SEPARATE processes that share one broker. If builders ever become threads in one host process, most
  of the `self/*` and `thread/*` families become genuinely intra-app and the census result flips."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

### [DD-SOB-SEQ-SEMANTICS] `seq` becomes bus-global rather than per-source — is that acceptable forever?

- decision: PROPOSED — accept it, and normalize `seq` out of the capture-diff ([CXC-SOB-WIRE-DELTAS]).
- rationale: no console consumer reads `seq`; `BusMessage.from_json` defaults it to 0; and the bus's
  own counter is the canonical behaviour for anything that publishes through the bus, so preserving
  per-source counters would mean re-introducing a per-source shadow of a bus concern.
- alternatives: (a) thread a per-source sequence through `publish_state` — rejected: it widens the bus
  API for a field nobody reads; (b) declare `seq` deprecated on the wire — a bigger change than this
  slice, belongs in [plt.cio:SPEC:CIO-BUS-PROTOCOL].
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify
- invalidating_assumption: "That no consumer uses `seq` for gap detection or replay ordering. The
  capture-replay surface ([plt.cio:SPEC:CIO-BUS-PROTOCOL] CR-*) is the one place that might; if replay
  ordering depends on a per-source monotonic `seq`, this reopens."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SOURCES-ON-BUS]

